/*
 * full_frame_row_blit.gq — Golden-test fixture locking in gq_draw_image()'s
 * new row-level uncompressed fast path (duplico/gamequeer#303,
 * HAL_oled_blit_row()) -- the batched replacement for #295's per-byte
 * HAL_oled_blit_byte() fast path.
 *
 * checker128.png is a purpose-built 128x128 asset (freshly generated, not
 * reused from checker.png -- see below for why size *and* content matter
 * here): bit (x + y + half) % 2, where half = 1 for y >= 64 and 0 otherwise
 * -- the same per-pixel-alternating adversarial density as checker.png
 * (every pixel differs from its horizontal/vertical neighbor, for the same
 * reason mask_encoding_a.gq's header gives: no "all-same-value run" slack
 * for a bit-order or byte-offset bug to hide in), *plus* a deliberate phase
 * flip at y = 64 (see below for why the flip matters, not just the size).
 *
 * Placed via bganim at the default (0, 0), full 128x128 -- i.e. exactly the
 * motivating case from issue #303 (a full-screen uncompressed frame): every
 * one of the image's 128 rows is byte-aligned, 16 bytes wide (128 / 8), and
 * entirely within the clip region, so every row takes the new
 * HAL_oled_blit_row() fast path from the very first pixel to the very last.
 *
 * Why 128x128 specifically (not reusing checker.png's 32x32, which
 * rowmajor_byte_blit.gq's bgsprite placement already incidentally exercises
 * the row path through): the image is 128 * 128 / 8 = 2048 bytes, exactly
 * two whole IMAGE_BUFFER_SIZE (1024-byte) cart-read chunks -- so the row
 * loop crosses an image_buffer reload boundary exactly once, between row 63
 * (the last row of chunk 1) and row 64 (the first row of chunk 2), landing
 * precisely on gq_draw_image()'s buffer-straddle guard's boundary
 * (IMAGE_BUFFER_SIZE / 16 bytes-per-row = 64 whole rows per chunk, so the
 * guard should never fall back to the byte-level path here). This
 * regression-locks the fast path's read-before-decode-advance ordering
 * (oled.c's comment on why HAL_oled_blit_row() is called *before*
 * gq_image_advance_byte() for a row): if that ordering were reversed, row
 * 63's write would read chunk 2's (already-reloaded) data instead of chunk
 * 1's.
 *
 * Why the y = 64 phase flip specifically (not just plain (x + y) % 2):
 * without it, this fixture initially passed even with the
 * read-before-decode ordering *reversed* -- a plain checkerboard has period
 * 2 in y, and 64 (the chunk boundary) is a multiple of 2, so chunk 1's row
 * 63 and chunk 2's row 63 (i.e. overall row 127) render pixel-identically,
 * making the corruption invisible. The half-image phase flip breaks that
 * accidental symmetry: reading the wrong chunk at the boundary now produces
 * a visibly different (phase-inverted) pattern.
 *
 * Ground truth from actually running the negative control (temporarily
 * reversing oled.c's HAL_oled_blit_row()/gq_image_advance_byte() call
 * order): this fixture fails, as designed -- but so do
 * golden_framebuffer_anim_advance_frame{0,1,2}. That's not a flaw in this
 * fixture's design; anim_advance.gq's bwcircles.gif frames turn out to
 * *also* be 128x128 uncompressed images (confirmed via --perf-dump:
 * draw_write count=128 for anim_advance.gqgame too), so they hit the exact
 * same buffer-straddle boundary at row 64 -- and unlike a plain checkerboard,
 * dithered circle content has no accidental period-2-in-y symmetry, so it
 * was already an incidental (if undocumented, pre-#303) negative control for
 * this ordering hazard. This fixture remains the dedicated, deliberately-
 * designed case (built specifically to guarantee -- not coincidentally
 * benefit from -- both the buffer-straddle boundary and the broken
 * y-shift symmetry, and to document why both are needed), not the only one
 * that catches it.
 *
 * How to reproduce the compiled fixture (full_frame_row_blit.gqgame):
 *
 *   From the gamequeer repo root, using the builder container:
 *     docker run --rm -v $(pwd):/workspaces/gamequeer --workdir /workspaces/gamequeer \
 *         --user $(id -u):$(id -g) gamequeer-builder:latest \
 *         /bin/bash -c "cd gamequeer/tests/golden && PYTHONPATH=/workspaces/gamequeer/gqc/src \
 *             python -m gqc compile --no-mem-map -o . full_frame_row_blit.gq"
 *
 *   Or via the Makefile target:
 *     make golden-fixture
 */

game {
    id = 1;
    title := "FullFrameRow";
    author := "test";
    starting_stage = blit_test;
}

animations {
    bgsprite <- "checker128.png" {
        w = 128;
        h = 128;
    }
}

stage blit_test {
    event enter {
        play bganim bgsprite;
    }
}
